      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2009 Wiley-Liss, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Three-year follow-up of major adverse cardiovascular event (MACE) (death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, target lesion revascularization) and the predictors of MACEs in diabetic patients after sirolimus-eluting stent (SES) or paclitaxel-eluting stent (PES) implantation have not been reported."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Diabetic patients with de novo coronary lesions (169 patients with 190 lesions) were randomly assigned prospectively to either SES or PES."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Baseline characteristics were similar between the two groups. The rates of MACEs [5.9% (n = 5) in the SES vs. 9.5% (n = 8) in the PES Group, P = 0.374] and definite stent thrombosis [1.2% (n = 1) in the SES vs. 3.6% (n = 3) in the PES Group, P = 0.368] were similar in the two groups during the three-year follow-up. Multivariate logistic analysis showed that insulin treatment was the only independent predictor of MACE [odds ratio (OR) 8.60, 95% confidence interval (CI) 3.25-22.76, P < 0.001] and target vessel revascularization (TVR) (OR 9.50, 95% CI 3.07-29.44, P < 0.001) during the three-year follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The rates of MACEs, TVR, and stent thrombosis during the three-year follow-up were similar in the SES and PES Groups. Insulin treatment was a main predictor of MACEs and TVR during the three-year follow-up after either SES or PES implantation."
          }
        ]
      },
